---
Part VI, Chapter 5: The language server
Package: packages/loom-lang/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The language server

The Loom language server is an Effect program. `startLanguageServer` runs it: it builds the pipeline layers once and captures a warm runtime, because Volar's hooks are synchronous and the language plugin runs the projection on that runtime. On `initialize` the server collects the plugins of the workspace the editor opened. It needs no compiler to start. A project's TypeScript is found, or fetched, the first time that project asks. From then on it serves every loom with the plugins of the loom's own workspace. It collects them the first time it meets a loom there, and again whenever that workspace's configuration changes. It also rereads a chapter whenever the chapter changes on disk while the editor has it closed, and whenever the editor closes it without saving, so an open loom never answers from text the chapter no longer holds.

Any editor that speaks the Language Server Protocol can start the server. VS Code starts it through the Loom extension, which loads a thin entry module. Neovim, Helix, Zed, and the JetBrains IDEs start it with the `loom lsp` command and talk to it over standard input and output. So the server lives in the CLI, the one tool every Loom user already has.

## Imports

The server is built from Volar's language-server toolkit over a `ManagedRuntime`. From the toolkit it takes the connection, the environment a language service runs in, and the types of a server, a project, a service plugin, a file the editor reports changed, and the language whose scripts a project projects. From Volar's language service it takes what a project is built from: `createLanguage`, which registers a project's scripts, `createUriMap`, which keys them, and `createLanguageService`, which runs the service plugins over them, with the types of a language service and of the snapshot a script holds. `FileChangeType` says whether a reported file was created, changed, or deleted. It imports `LoomCompiler`, the projector and cache the runtime warms, with the `SummaryEdit` that keeps a chapter box in step, and `LoomFormatter`, which formats a file on it, with the `DocumentSource`, `WorkspaceSettings`, and `LoomConfig` they require, and the two halves this module registers, `loomLanguagePlugin` and `loomServicePlugins`. `LanguagePackages` holds the language packages each workspace activates, so the compiler can ask them to write the path a reference names, as the chapter "Referring to modules" describes. From the workspace source come the `WorkspaceSource` service, which fetches a workspace's looms, its `FileSystemSource` layer, and the `Workspace` its `open` method hands over. From the language store comes `workspaceOf`, which finds the nested workspace a file belongs to, `standalone`, which says whether Loom runs as the standalone executable, and what the server needs to fetch TypeScript 7: `fetchTypeScript`, which fetches it, `typeScriptVersion`, the version it fetches, and `fetchedTypeScriptDir` and `fetchedExecutable`, which say where it lands. The service contract names the `TypescriptSdkApi` the server lends, the `FetchedTypeScript` within it, and the `ClassicTypeScriptMissing` its search for TypeScript 6 fails with.

Effect's `Schema` reads the editor's initialization options, `Option` carries a compiler or a workspace that may not be there, `Result` carries a compiler or the reason there is none, and `Data` types the failure of a fetch. `Cache` holds what the server builds for each workspace, and `Array` gathers across them. `Semaphore` holds back a request while the server reprojects the open looms, and `Ref` holds the looms the editor has changed since the last request, and the text the editor last held for each open loom. `Scheduler` keeps the fiber that rebuilds them from yielding. A `Source` is what the compiler reads a loom's text through. Node's `createRequire` finds an installed `typescript` package the way Node itself would, and loads it. `existsSync` checks that a folder holds a compiler, and `statSync` reads when a loom was last saved. `stringSnapshot` turns a loom's text into the snapshot a script holds. `join` builds the path a search starts from, and `dirname` trims a file's path to the directory that holds it. `URI` is the address Volar names a document by, and it parses the addresses the editor reports.

=>

import {
  Array,
  Cache,
  Data,
  Effect,
  Exit,
  Layer,
  ManagedRuntime,
  Match,
  Option,
  Result,
  Schema,
  Ref,
  Scheduler,
  Semaphore,
  pipe,
} from 'effect'
import {
  createConnection,
  createLanguageServiceEnvironment,
  createServer,
  FileChangeType,
  type FileEvent,
  type Language,
  type LanguageServer,
  type LanguageServerProject,
  type LanguageServicePlugin,
} from '@volar/language-server/node'
import {
  createLanguage,
  createLanguageService,
  createUriMap,
  type IScriptSnapshot,
  type LanguageService,
} from '@volar/language-service'
import { existsSync, statSync } from 'node:fs'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import {
  LoomCompiler,
  DocumentSource,
  stringSnapshot,
  type SummaryEdit,
  within,
} from './LoomCompiler'
import { LanguagePackages } from './LanguagePackages'
import { type ReadError, type Source } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import { LoomFormatter } from './LoomFormatter'
import { WorkspaceSettings } from './WorkspaceSettings'
import { LoomConfig } from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import {
  fetchedExecutable,
  fetchedTypeScriptDir,
  fetchTypeScript,
  standalone,
  typeScriptVersion,
  workspaceOf,
} from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LoomStore'
import {
  ClassicTypeScriptMissing,
  type FetchedTypeScript,
  type TypescriptSdkApi,
} from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LanguageService'
import { loomLanguagePlugin, loomServicePlugins } from './LoomLanguagePlugin'
import { Environment, type Workspace, WorkspaceSource } from './WorkspaceSource'

## The compilers a project may ask for

The server checks no code itself, so it needs no compiler to start. The TypeScript service checks a project's code on the compiler the project chose, and asks the `TypescriptSdk` capability for that compiler the first time the project needs it. The server builds the capability, because only the server knows what the editor sent and where Loom itself is installed. A workspace with no TypeScript project never loads a compiler at all.

A project that installs TypeScript 6 checks against a TypeScript 6 loaded into the server's own process. Such a compiler comes from a TypeScript SDK directory, which Volar calls the tsdk: the `lib` directory of a `typescript` package, where `typescript.js` sits beside the standard library's declaration files.

VS Code supplies one. The Loom extension sends the path of the TypeScript that VS Code ships, in the `typescript.tsdk` field of its initialization options. `EditorTsdkSchema` is the shape of that field, and `editorTsdk` decodes it from whatever the client sent, yielding nothing when the field is absent or malformed, or when the folder it names holds no `typescript.js`.

=>

const EditorTsdkSchema = Schema.Struct({
  typescript: Schema.Struct({ tsdk: Schema.String }),
})

const editorTsdk = (options: unknown): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Schema.decodeUnknownOption(EditorTsdkSchema)(options),
    Option.map((decoded) => decoded.typescript.tsdk),
    Option.filter((tsdk) => existsSync(join(tsdk, 'typescript.js'))),
  )

~

Every other editor starts `loom lsp` with no such option, so the server looks for the compiler itself. What it looks for is TypeScript's JavaScript API, the `lib/typescript.js` file that TypeScript 6 and every earlier release ship. TypeScript 7 is a native program, and its package holds no such file.

`installedTsdk` resolves `typescript/lib/typescript.js` the way Node's `require` would from a given file, walking up its `node_modules` directories, and returns the directory that holds it. It yields nothing when the walk finds no TypeScript, and nothing when the TypeScript it finds is version 7, so the search moves on to the next place.

=>

const installedTsdk = (from: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.map(
    Option.liftThrowable(() => createRequire(from).resolve('typescript/lib/typescript.js'))(),
    dirname,
  )

~

`classicTsdk` tries three places in order for a project's folder and takes the first that holds a compiler. The project's folder comes first, because the project's own install always wins: every editor, VS Code among them, checks a project with the TypeScript 6 the project builds with. A project on TypeScript 6 installs one of its own, so the places after it serve only an install the server cannot read. The editor's own path comes second.

Loom's own install comes third: the directories above the server's module, which hold `typescript` when Loom is installed beside it. Only the ES-module bundle that `loom lsp` runs knows its own location. The CommonJS bundles, the VS Code extension's `server.js` and the `loom-language-server` bin, compile `import.meta` to an empty object, so the search finds nothing there, and VS Code never needs it. The standalone executable skips this place, since nothing is installed beside it. Its modules live inside the executable, and a search that starts there would walk up from the working directory instead.

=>

const besideLoom = (): Option.Option<string> =>
  standalone ? Option.none() : installedTsdk(import.meta.url)

const classicTsdk = (options: unknown, folder: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    installedTsdk(join(folder, 'package.json')),
    Option.orElse(() => editorTsdk(options)),
    Option.orElse(besideLoom),
  )

~

`classicTypeScript` loads the compiler from the directory `classicTsdk` finds, with `require`. It loads each compiler once and keeps it for the server's life in `loaded`, keyed by its directory, so every project on the same TypeScript 6 shares one copy. When no place holds a TypeScript 6, or the compiler fails to load, it fails with a `ClassicTypeScriptMissing`, and the project's TypeScript features report it. Nothing else is tried in its place.

=>

type TypeScript = typeof import('typescript')

export const classicTypeScript = (
  options: unknown,
): ((folder: string) => Result.Result<TypeScript, ClassicTypeScriptMissing>) => {
  const loaded = new Map<string, TypeScript>()
  const load = (folder: string, tsdk: string): Result.Result<TypeScript, ClassicTypeScriptMissing> =>
    Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(loaded.get(tsdk)), {
      onSome: (ts) => Result.succeed(ts),
      onNone: () =>
        pipe(
          Result.try((): TypeScript => {
            const file = join(tsdk, 'typescript.js')
            return createRequire(file)(file)
          }),
          Result.map((ts) => {
            loaded.set(tsdk, ts)
            return ts
          }),
          Result.mapError(
            (cause) => new ClassicTypeScriptMissing({ folder, tsdk: Option.some(tsdk), cause }),
          ),
        ),
    })
  return (folder) =>
    Option.match(classicTsdk(options, folder), {
      onNone: () => Result.fail(new ClassicTypeScriptMissing({ folder, tsdk: Option.none() })),
      onSome: (tsdk) => load(folder, tsdk),
    })
}

~

A project that installs no TypeScript checks against the TypeScript 7 Loom fetches, at the version Loom pins, through the store's `fetchTypeScript`. `fetchedTypeScript` describes that compiler for the capability. Its `executable` is where the fetch puts it, so a project can be assigned to it before it is on the machine.

Its `fetch` runs the fetch on the server's runtime the first time any project asks. `Effect.cached` keeps the outcome, so every later call shares that one fetch, and the editor downloads TypeScript 7 once however many projects ask at the same moment. A machine that holds it already answers at once. The store writes a line to the log whenever it downloads, naming the version and the folder. When the fetch fails, every later call fails the same way, with a `TypeScriptNotFetched` that names the version, the folder and the cause, until the server restarts. A failed download is not tried again on every keystroke.

=>

export class TypeScriptNotFetched extends Data.TaggedError('TypeScriptNotFetched')<{
  readonly version: string
  readonly folder: string
  readonly cause: unknown
}> {
  get message(): string {
    return `could not fetch TypeScript ${this.version} into ${this.folder}: ${String(this.cause)}`
  }
}

const fetchedTypeScript: Effect.Effect<FetchedTypeScript> = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const folder = fetchedTypeScriptDir(typeScriptVersion)
  const once = yield* Effect.cached(
    fetchTypeScript(typeScriptVersion).pipe(
      Effect.mapError(
        (error) =>
          new TypeScriptNotFetched({ version: typeScriptVersion, folder, cause: error.cause }),
      ),
    ),
  )
  return {
    version: typeScriptVersion,
    executable: fetchedExecutable(typeScriptVersion),
    fetch: () => runtime.runPromise(Effect.asVoid(once)),
  }
})

~

`typescriptSdkFor` assembles the capability from the editor's initialization options. The server builds it once, and every workspace's services share it.

=>

const typescriptSdkFor = (options: unknown): Effect.Effect<TypescriptSdkApi> =>
  Effect.map(fetchedTypeScript, (fetched) => ({ classic: classicTypeScript(options), fetched }))

## Every loom answers to its own workspace

A workspace decides which languages its looms use and where their services come from. Its `config.yaml` names the active languages, and its store under `.loom/services/` holds the packages that serve them. Workspaces can nest. A Git worktree checked out inside a repository carries a `.loom/config.yaml` of its own. An editor opens one folder, but the looms it shows may belong to several workspaces.

So the server serves each loom with the services of the workspace the loom belongs to, not those of the folder the editor opened. A loom in a nested workspace is checked and formatted with that workspace's languages, at the versions its own store holds. `loom format` loads the same services for the same loom. Whether the editor opened the outer folder or the nested one makes no difference to the loom.

`workspaceFor` names the workspace a document belongs to. For a file on disk it is the nearest directory above the file that holds a `.loom/config.yaml`, the one `workspaceOf` finds. A document outside every workspace, or one that is not a file on disk, belongs to `home`. That is the workspace of the folder the editor opened, or the folder itself when it lies in no workspace.

=>

const workspaceFor = (home: string, uri: URI): string =>
  uri.scheme === 'file' ? Option.getOrElse(workspaceOf(dirname(uri.fsPath)), () => home) : home

~

Volar already has a seam for this. For every request, Volar asks a project which language service answers the document, and a language service carries its own list of service plugins. So a project per workspace, built over that workspace's plugins, serves each loom with the services of its own workspace.

Volar ships two kinds of project, and Loom uses neither. Its TypeScript project builds a language service for each `tsconfig.json` it finds, and loads a TypeScript compiler to find them. A Loom workspace is the folder of its `.loom/config.yaml`, not of a `tsconfig.json`, and the TypeScript service finds each project's `tsconfig.json` itself, so that compiler would be loaded for nothing. Volar's simple project loads no compiler, but it knows only the documents the editor has open. An answer often lands in a chapter the editor has closed: the definition of a name in another chapter's code, or a reference there. Volar maps such an answer back to its place in the `.loom` only when it can project the closed chapter, and the simple project cannot.

So `projectOver` builds a project of Loom's own for one workspace. It holds one language service over the workspace's plugins, which `languageServiceOver` builds, and `reload` disposes it and builds it again. The language behind it reads each document from the editor, as the simple project's does. A loom the editor has not opened it fetches through `savedLoom`, when Volar asks for files on disk. The project's looms project through the Loom language plugin, on the one warm runtime every workspace shares.

=>

const languageServiceOver = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  plugins: ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>,
  savedLoom: (uri: URI) => Option.Option<IScriptSnapshot>,
): LanguageService => {
  const language: Language<URI> = createLanguage<URI>(
    [{ getLanguageId: (uri) => server.documents.get(uri)?.languageId }, loomLanguagePlugin(runtime)],
    createUriMap(false),
    (uri, includeFsFiles) =>
      Option.match(
        Option.orElse(
          Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(server.documents.get(uri)), (document) =>
            document.getSnapshot(),
          ),
          () => (includeFsFiles ? savedLoom(uri) : Option.none()),
        ),
        {
          onSome: (snapshot) => void language.scripts.set(uri, snapshot),
          onNone: () => language.scripts.delete(uri),
        },
      ),
  )
  return createLanguageService(
    language,
    [...plugins],
    createLanguageServiceEnvironment(server, server.workspaceFolders.all),
    {},
  )
}

export const projectOver = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  plugins: ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>,
): LanguageServerProject => {
  const savedLoom = savedLooms()
  const state = { service: languageServiceOver(server, plugins, savedLoom) }
  return {
    setup: () => undefined,
    getLanguageService: () => state.service,
    getExistingLanguageServices: () => [state.service],
    reload: () => {
      state.service.dispose()
      state.service = languageServiceOver(server, plugins, savedLoom)
    },
  }
}

~

`fetchSaved` fetches a loom's saved text through the `WorkspaceSource` service and hands it to the `DocumentSource` service to hold, so the compiler reads the same text the server just fetched. When storage no longer has the loom, `fetchSaved` has `DocumentSource` forget it and yields nothing.

`savedLooms` builds the reader that `projectOver` calls `savedLoom`. It fetches a loom through `fetchSaved` and keeps its snapshot until the file's modified time moves. A save the editor never reports still moves that time, so the server sees the saved text even from an editor that sends no change notifications. Volar asks for a script each time it needs one, and a fresh snapshot is a new version to Volar, so a loom read afresh on every ask would be projected afresh on every ask. A chapter read once is projected once. A file that is no loom, or that is no longer on disk, yields nothing, and the language forgets it.

A loom kept anywhere but on a disk has no modified time. `onDisk` reads a loom whose address is a file, and `inMemory` answers any other loom from the text the `DocumentSource` service holds, through `heldText`. It keeps the snapshot until that text changes, so a remote chapter is projected once too. A loom the source never handed over would have to be fetched, and a fetch cannot finish inside Volar's synchronous call, so `runSyncExit` reports the failure and the loom yields nothing.

=>

interface SavedLoom {
  readonly modified: number
  readonly snapshot: IScriptSnapshot
}

const fetchSaved = (
  path: string,
): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>, never, WorkspaceSource | DocumentSource> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const source = yield* WorkspaceSource
    const documents = yield* DocumentSource
    const saved = yield* Effect.option(source.read(path))
    yield* Option.match(saved, {
      onNone: () => documents.forget(path),
      onSome: (text) => documents.hold(new Map([[path, text]])),
    })
    return saved
  })

interface ShownLoom {
  readonly text: string
  readonly snapshot: IScriptSnapshot
}

const heldText = (uri: URI): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>, never, WorkspaceSource | DocumentSource> =>
  Effect.all([WorkspaceSource, DocumentSource]).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(([source, documents]) => Effect.option(documents.read(source.locationOf(uri)))),
  )

const savedLooms = (): ((uri: URI) => Option.Option<IScriptSnapshot>) => {
  const read = new Map<string, SavedLoom>()
  const shown = new Map<string, ShownLoom>()
  const readAgain = (path: string, modified: number): Option.Option<IScriptSnapshot> =>
    Option.map(runtime.runSync(fetchSaved(path)), (text) => {
      const snapshot = stringSnapshot(text)
      read.set(path, { modified, snapshot })
      return snapshot
    })
  const onDisk = (uri: URI): Option.Option<IScriptSnapshot> =>
    pipe(
      Option.liftThrowable(() => statSync(uri.fsPath).mtimeMs)(),
      Option.flatMap((modified) =>
        pipe(
          Option.fromNullishOr(read.get(uri.fsPath)),
          Option.filter((held) => held.modified === modified),
          Option.map((held) => held.snapshot),
          Option.orElse(() => readAgain(uri.fsPath, modified)),
        ),
      ),
    )
  const inMemory = (uri: URI): Option.Option<IScriptSnapshot> =>
    Option.map(
      Exit.match(runtime.runSyncExit(heldText(uri)), {
        onFailure: () => Option.none<string>(),
        onSuccess: (text) => text,
      }),
      (text) =>
        pipe(
          Option.fromNullishOr(shown.get(uri.toString())),
          Option.filter((kept) => kept.text === text),
          Option.map((kept) => kept.snapshot),
          Option.getOrElse(() => {
            const snapshot = stringSnapshot(text)
            shown.set(uri.toString(), { text, snapshot })
            return snapshot
          }),
        ),
    )
  return (uri) =>
    Match.value(uri).pipe(
      Match.when((loom) => !isLoom(loom), () => Option.none<IScriptSnapshot>()),
      Match.when((loom) => loom.scheme === 'file', onDisk),
      Match.orElse(inMemory),
    )
}

~

`workspaceProject` is the project the server hands to Volar. Its `projects` cache holds one project per workspace, the project `projectOver` builds. Its `services` cache holds the plugins each project is built over. `getLanguageService` answers a document from the project of the document's workspace. It first takes the `reprojecting` semaphore, waiting while a reprojection holds it, and settles the edits the editor has made since the last request. `getExistingLanguageServices` settles them too, then reaches every project the `projects` cache holds, as `reload` does. Volar sets the project up with the server once the editor has initialized. The `projects` cache already builds every workspace's project with that same server, so `setup` has two tasks left. It starts `watchConfigs`, which keeps both caches and the builds of each workspace's looms in step with its configuration and its compiler options, and `watchLooms`, which keeps every open loom in step with the closed chapters it reads.

=>

export const workspaceProject = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  services: Cache.Cache<string, ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  reprojecting: Semaphore.Semaphore,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): LanguageServerProject => ({
  setup: (server) => {
    watchConfigs(server, home, services, projects, edited)
    watchLooms(server, home, projects, reprojecting, edited)
  },
  getLanguageService: (uri) =>
    runtime.runPromise(
      reprojecting.withPermits(1)(settleEdits(server, home, projects, edited)).pipe(
        Effect.andThen(Cache.get(projects, workspaceFor(home, uri))),
        Effect.flatMap((project) => Effect.promise(async () => project.getLanguageService(uri))),
      ),
    ),
  getExistingLanguageServices: () =>
    runtime.runPromise(
      reprojecting.withPermits(1)(settleEdits(server, home, projects, edited)).pipe(
        Effect.andThen(Cache.values(projects)),
        Effect.flatMap((all) =>
          Effect.forEach(all, (project) =>
            Effect.promise(async () => (await project.getExistingLanguageServices()) ?? []),
          ),
        ),
        Effect.map(Array.flatten),
      ),
    ),
  reload: () =>
    runtime.runSync(
      Cache.values(projects).pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((all) =>
          Effect.forEach(all, (project) => Effect.sync(() => project.reload()), { discard: true }),
        ),
      ),
    ),
})

~

A workspace's configuration can change while the server runs. `loom add` installs a language's service in the store and then rewrites `config.yaml`. `loom remove` takes the service out of the store and rewrites `config.yaml` too. So a change to `config.yaml` is the one sign that a workspace's services changed. `watchConfigs` asks the editor to watch every `.loom/config.yaml` and listens for the changes the editor reports. `reconfiguredWorkspaces` reads a batch of changed files and returns the workspaces whose configuration changed, each the folder that holds a changed `.loom/` folder.

`forgetWorkspace` drops what the server built for one workspace. It reloads the workspace's project through `reloadProject`, which disposes the project's language service and the plugins inside it, then removes the workspace from both caches. The next request for one of the workspace's looms collects its plugins afresh, from the configuration and the store as they now stand, and builds a new project over them. Under Node, a service `loom add` replaced comes back at its new version, because the loader imports each version at an address of its own.

A configuration also decides how each of the workspace's looms is built. It names the anchor delimiters a loom uses, its primary language, and the variables its value warps read. The compiler's build cache holds every loom it built under the old configuration, and the cache would hand those builds back unchanged. So `rebuildUnder` evicts every build within the workspace through the compiler's `invalidateWithin`. A closed loom is built again from disk the next time a composition reads it. An open loom may hold unsaved edits, and a composition that missed it in the cache would read it from disk. So `rebuildUnder` also notes as edited every open loom the eviction reached, and the next request rebuilds each one from the editor's text before anything reads it. It takes them by the same rule the eviction uses, the compiler's `within`, because the eviction reaches past the workspace into any workspace nested inside it. `openLooms` lists every loom the editor has open. The build cache on disk needs nothing, because it keys each build by the settings it was built under.

`reconfigure` rebuilds and forgets every workspace a batch of changes names. It then asks Volar to refresh, clearing the diagnostics on screen, so every open loom is checked again with the services it now has.

A `tsconfig.json` or a `jsconfig.json` changes no service, but it may change the compiler options a TypeScript 6 program checks with, which the TypeScript service read when it built the program. A `package.json` may change the compiler itself. Installing TypeScript into a project rewrites its `package.json`, and the TypeScript service chose each project's compiler when it first met the project, so a project that installs TypeScript after the editor opened it would keep the TypeScript 7 Loom fetched.

`retypedWorkspaces` returns the workspaces a batch of such changes reaches, each the workspace the changed file belongs to. `isCompilerSetting` tells such a file by its name, and leaves out every file under `node_modules`, which an install rewrites by the thousand. `retype` reloads each workspace's project and asks Volar to refresh, so the next request chooses each project's compiler again and builds the TypeScript service's programs afresh. A TypeScript 7 program restarts its server in the same way, and loads the workspace again. `watchConfigs` asks the editor to watch all four kinds of file.

=>

const reconfiguredWorkspaces = (
  source: WorkspaceSource['Service'],
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  pipe(
    changes,
    Array.filter((change) => change.uri.endsWith('/.loom/config.yaml')),
    Array.map((change) => dirname(dirname(source.locationOf(URI.parse(change.uri))))),
    Array.dedupe,
  )

const reloadProject = (
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  workspace: string,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Cache.getSuccess(projects, workspace).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(
      Option.match({
        onNone: () => Effect.void,
        onSome: (project) => Effect.sync(() => project.reload()),
      }),
    ),
  )

const forgetWorkspace = (
  services: Cache.Cache<string, ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  workspace: string,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  reloadProject(projects, workspace).pipe(
    Effect.andThen(Cache.invalidate(projects, workspace)),
    Effect.andThen(Cache.invalidate(services, workspace)),
  )

export const rebuildUnder = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
  workspace: string,
): Effect.Effect<void, never, LoomCompiler | WorkspaceSource> =>
  Effect.all([LoomCompiler, WorkspaceSource]).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(([compiler, source]) =>
      compiler.invalidateWithin(workspace).pipe(
        Effect.andThen(
          Effect.forEach(
            Array.filter(openLooms(server), (uri) => within(workspace, source.locationOf(uri))),
            (uri) => recordEdit(edited, uri),
            { discard: true },
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

const reconfigure = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  services: Cache.Cache<string, ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
  workspaces: ReadonlyArray<string>,
): Effect.Effect<void, never, LoomCompiler | WorkspaceSource> =>
  workspaces.length === 0
    ? Effect.void
    : Effect.forEach(
        workspaces,
        (workspace) =>
          rebuildUnder(server, edited, workspace).pipe(
            Effect.andThen(forgetWorkspace(services, projects, workspace)),
          ),
        { discard: true },
      ).pipe(Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => void server.languageFeatures.requestRefresh(true))))

const isCompilerSetting = (uri: string): boolean =>
  /\/(tsconfig|jsconfig|package)\.json$/.test(uri) && !uri.includes('/node_modules/')

const retypedWorkspaces = (
  home: string,
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  pipe(
    changes,
    Array.filter((change) => isCompilerSetting(change.uri)),
    Array.map((change) => workspaceFor(home, URI.parse(change.uri))),
    Array.dedupe,
  )

const retype = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  workspaces: ReadonlyArray<string>,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  workspaces.length === 0
    ? Effect.void
    : Effect.forEach(workspaces, (workspace) => reloadProject(projects, workspace), {
        discard: true,
      }).pipe(Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => void server.languageFeatures.requestRefresh(true))))

const watchConfigs = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  services: Cache.Cache<string, ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): void => {
  void server.fileWatcher.watchFiles([
    '**/.loom/config.yaml',
    '**/tsconfig.json',
    '**/jsconfig.json',
    '**/package.json',
  ])
  server.fileWatcher.onDidChangeWatchedFiles(({ changes }) =>
    runtime.runSync(
      WorkspaceSource.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((source) =>
          reconfigure(server, services, projects, edited, reconfiguredWorkspaces(source, changes)),
        ),
        Effect.andThen(retype(server, projects, retypedWorkspaces(home, changes))),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

~

One limit remains, and it concerns capabilities. The editor learns the server's capabilities once, from the answer to `initialize`, and Volar derives that answer from the plugins of `home`. A nested workspace's plugins receive only the requests those capabilities let the editor send. So do the plugins of a language activated after the server started. The Loom language is active in every workspace, and its plugins already claim diagnostics, navigation, rename, colouring, and formatting. So the limit reaches only a feature that no plugin of `home` claimed at `initialize`, such as hover in a workspace whose `home` activates no TypeScript.

## A chapter changes behind the editor's back

The editor reports every change to a loom it has open, keystroke by keystroke. A chapter the editor has closed changes too, and nothing the editor does reports it. `git pull` and `git checkout` rewrite chapters, an agent edits them, and `loom toc` rewrites the book. An open loom still reads those chapters. Its anchors resolve against the sections a closed chapter holds, and its code imports the files a closed chapter tangles.

The compiler's build cache keeps every chapter the server has read, the closed ones included. Left alone, the server would answer from the text a closed chapter held when it first read it. An open loom's code would be checked against the types a closed chapter used to export, and go-to-definition would land where a section used to be.

So `watchLooms` asks the editor to watch every `.loom` file, as `watchConfigs` watches every `config.yaml`. `isLoom` tells a loom's address by its path. `closedLoomChanges` reads a batch of reported changes and keeps those to looms the editor does not have open. The editor's own text governs an open loom, so a change on disk to an open loom evicts nothing.

The server keeps the text `DocumentSource` holds in step with storage. `fetchReported` fetches every loom a batch reports, open or closed, through `fetchSaved`. The editor's own text still governs an open loom, but once the editor closes it, the compiler reads what storage holds.

`rereadLoom` evicts one changed loom from the build cache with the compiler's `invalidate`, so the next composition reads the text `fetchReported` has just handed to `DocumentSource`. It then decides whether the change can reach an open loom. A changed loom the cache holds can, since some composition read it. A changed loom the cache does not hold cannot, since no composition holds its old text. A new loom can, because the next composition finds it when it lists the corpus, and an anchor that named nothing may now resolve. A deleted loom always counts, because Volar may still hold its script. `rereadLoom` returns the change when it counts, and nothing otherwise. It names a loom by the location the `WorkspaceSource` service's `locationOf` method gives the loom's address, as the language plugin does, so both evict the same entry of the build cache. Every crossing from an address to a location goes through that method, and every crossing back through `uriOf`. Two checks are rules about folders and keep reading a file address's `fsPath` behind a test of its `file` scheme: `workspaceFor`, which finds the folder of a nested workspace, and `savedLooms`, which reads a file's modified time.

=>

const isLoom = (uri: URI): boolean => uri.path.endsWith('.loom')

const closedLoomChanges = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
): ReadonlyArray<FileEvent> =>
  Array.filter(changes, (change) => {
    const uri = URI.parse(change.uri)
    return isLoom(uri) && server.documents.get(uri) === undefined
  })

const rereadLoom = (
  compiler: LoomCompiler['Service'],
  source: WorkspaceSource['Service'],
  change: FileEvent,
): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<FileEvent>> =>
  compiler
    .invalidate(source.locationOf(URI.parse(change.uri)))
    .pipe(
      Effect.map((reprojected) =>
        change.type === FileChangeType.Changed && reprojected.length === 0
          ? Option.none()
          : Option.some(change),
      ),
    )

const fetchReported = (
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
): Effect.Effect<void, never, WorkspaceSource | DocumentSource> =>
  WorkspaceSource.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((source) =>
      Effect.forEach(
        Array.filter(Array.map(changes, (change) => URI.parse(change.uri)), isLoom),
        (uri) => fetchSaved(source.locationOf(uri)),
        { discard: true },
      ),
    ),
  )

const rereadLooms = (
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>, never, LoomCompiler | WorkspaceSource> =>
  Effect.all([LoomCompiler, WorkspaceSource]).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(([compiler, source]) =>
      Effect.forEach(changes, (change) => rereadLoom(compiler, source, change)),
    ),
    Effect.map(Array.getSomes),
  )

~

`rereadLooms` rereads every loom in a batch and keeps the changes that count.

Evicting a chapter is not enough for the looms the editor has open. Each open loom holds a projection, built when the loom last changed, with the sections it drew in from other chapters already inlined. Volar also keeps the diagnostics a service reports for a projection until the projection changes, whenever the service declares that its diagnostics read no other file. The TypeScript service declares exactly that. So every open loom a change can reach must be projected again.

Volar has a flag for this. A script whose `isAssociationDirty` is set is projected again on the next request for it, through the language plugin's `updateVirtualCode`, from the text the editor holds. Volar sets the flag itself when a script a loom depends on changes, but only for a loom that draws the script in by an anchor. A loom that imports a closed chapter's file through its code holds no such link, so the server marks every open loom of the workspace instead. `markStale` sets the flag on one open loom's script. The reprojection keeps the loom's own build, because the loom's text has not changed, so an unsaved edit survives it. Only the evicted chapter is read from disk again.

Reading a loom's script from a language service creates the script there if it is missing, and projects the loom again. So `markStale` reaches each open loom through the language service the project answers it from, and never asks a language service that does not hold the loom.

A deleted chapter leaves its script behind in Volar, with the projection it last held, and no request asks for that script again. `forgetScript` deletes it from every language service the project reports, since any of them may hold it. Deleting a script that is not there does nothing.

`refreshProject` brings one workspace's project up to date. It forgets the script of each loom it is handed, then marks every open loom of the workspace stale. `openLoomsIn` lists those open looms. A step that fails for one loom logs its cause through `logged`, and the pass moves on to the next. `reprojectWorkspace` refreshes the project of one workspace. A workspace whose project the server has not built yet holds no projections, so it needs nothing.

=>

const openLooms = (server: LanguageServer): ReadonlyArray<URI> =>
  pipe(
    server.documents.all(),
    Array.map((document) => URI.parse(document.uri)),
    Array.filter(isLoom),
  )

const openLoomsIn = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  workspace: string,
): ReadonlyArray<URI> =>
  Array.filter(openLooms(server), (uri) => workspaceFor(home, uri) === workspace)

const logged = (step: string, uri: URI, effect: Effect.Effect<void>): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  effect.pipe(
    Effect.catchCause((cause) => Effect.logError(`loom: ${step} ${uri.toString()} failed`, cause)),
  )

const markStale = (project: LanguageServerProject, uri: URI): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.promise(async () => project.getLanguageService(uri)).pipe(
    Effect.map((languageService) => languageService.context.language.scripts.get(uri)),
    Effect.flatMap((script) =>
      Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(script), {
        onNone: () => Effect.void,
        onSome: (stale) =>
          Effect.sync(() => {
            stale.isAssociationDirty = true
          }),
      }),
    ),
  )

const forgetScript = (language: Language<URI>, uri: URI): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.sync(() => language.scripts.delete(uri))

export const refreshProject = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  workspace: string,
  project: LanguageServerProject,
  forgotten: ReadonlyArray<URI>,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const languageServices = yield* Effect.promise(
      async () => (await project.getExistingLanguageServices()) ?? [],
    )
    yield* Effect.forEach(
      Array.cartesian(languageServices, forgotten),
      ([languageService, uri]) =>
        logged('forgetting', uri, forgetScript(languageService.context.language, uri)),
      { discard: true },
    )
    yield* Effect.forEach(
      openLoomsIn(server, home, workspace),
      (uri) => logged('reprojecting', uri, markStale(project, uri)),
      { discard: true },
    )
  })

const reprojectWorkspace = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  workspace: string,
  forgotten: ReadonlyArray<URI>,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Cache.getSuccess(projects, workspace).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(
      Option.match({
        onNone: () => Effect.void,
        onSome: (project) => refreshProject(server, home, workspace, project, forgotten),
      }),
    ),
  )

~

`reproject` brings up to date the project of every workspace the counted changes belong to. It forgets the script of every deleted loom, and of any other loom the caller names. Then it asks Volar to refresh, so the editor asks again for every open loom's diagnostics. A batch with no change that counts leaves every projection as it is.

=>

const reproject = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
  alsoForgotten: ReadonlyArray<URI>,
): Effect.Effect<void> => {
  const forgotten = [
    ...pipe(
      changes,
      Array.filter((change) => change.type === FileChangeType.Deleted),
      Array.map((change) => URI.parse(change.uri)),
    ),
    ...alsoForgotten,
  ]
  return changes.length === 0
    ? Effect.void
    : Effect.forEach(
        Array.dedupe(Array.map(changes, (change) => workspaceFor(home, URI.parse(change.uri)))),
        (workspace) => reprojectWorkspace(server, home, projects, workspace, forgotten),
        { discard: true },
      ).pipe(
        Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => void server.languageFeatures.requestRefresh(false))),
      )
}

~

A loom the editor closes can leave the build cache behind it too. While the loom was open, the cache held the text the loom had in the editor. When the editor closes it without saving, the file on disk still holds the text it had before. Nothing on disk changed, so the editor reports nothing. Yet every open loom that draws the closed one in now draws in text that exists nowhere.

`closeOf` fetches the loom's saved text through `fetchSaved`, compares it with the text the editor held, and turns a close into the change it amounts to. A loom closed with its text saved amounts to no change at all, and the server does nothing. A loom closed with unsaved text amounts to a change on disk. The server rereads it, and forgets the script Volar built from the unsaved text. A loom that storage no longer has, because it was deleted while the editor held it open, amounts to a deletion.

=>

export const closeOf = (
  uri: URI,
  text: string,
): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>, never, WorkspaceSource | DocumentSource> =>
  WorkspaceSource.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((source) => fetchSaved(source.locationOf(uri))),
    Effect.map(
      Option.match({
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<FileEvent> => [
          { uri: uri.toString(), type: FileChangeType.Deleted },
        ],
        onSome: (saved) =>
          saved === text ? [] : [{ uri: uri.toString(), type: FileChangeType.Changed }],
      }),
    ),
  )

~

`rereadOnDisk` runs the whole pass in one fiber, forked on the warm runtime. The pass fetches each changed loom, evicts it, and reprojects the open looms. A fetch from remote storage waits on the network, and the reprojection asks a project for its language services, which answers with a promise, so no part of the pass can run synchronously inside the handler. The fiber holds the `reprojecting` semaphore while it runs. A forked fiber starts at once, so the pass takes the semaphore before the handler returns, and so before the server reads another request. The project the server hands to Volar waits on that semaphore before it answers any request. So no request is answered from a loom's old text between the change and the reprojection. A pass that fails logs its cause, so a failure never passes silently.

`rereadOnDisk` takes the fetch as an Effect that yields the changes to reread, and a function that names the scripts the changes leave behind. `watchLooms` runs it on every batch the editor reports, with `fetchReported` as the fetch, and on every loom the editor closes, with `closeOf`. A close that amounts to a change also forgets the script Volar built from the unsaved text. `watchLooms` also notes every loom the editor opens or changes, for the next request to settle, and hands each change to `keepInStep`, which the section "A box kept in step as the author types" describes, and `forgetSeen` drops the text it remembered for a loom the editor closes.

=>

const rereadOnDisk = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  reprojecting: Semaphore.Semaphore,
  fetched: Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>, never, WorkspaceSource | DocumentSource>,
  forgottenBy: (changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>) => ReadonlyArray<URI>,
): void =>
  void runtime.runFork(
    reprojecting.withPermits(1)(
      fetched.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((changes) =>
          rereadLooms(changes).pipe(
            Effect.flatMap((counted) =>
              reproject(server, home, projects, counted, forgottenBy(changes)),
            ),
          ),
        ),
        Effect.catchCause((cause) => Effect.logError('loom: rereading changed looms failed', cause)),
      ),
    ),
  )

const watchLooms = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  reprojecting: Semaphore.Semaphore,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): void => {
  void server.fileWatcher.watchFiles(['**/*.loom'])
  const seen = runtime.runSync(Ref.make<ReadonlyMap<string, string>>(new Map()))
  server.documents.onDidChangeContent(({ document }) => {
    runtime.runSync(recordEdit(edited, URI.parse(document.uri)))
    keepInStep(
      server,
      home,
      projects,
      reprojecting,
      edited,
      seen,
      URI.parse(document.uri),
      document.getText(),
    )
  })
  server.fileWatcher.onDidChangeWatchedFiles(({ changes }) =>
    rereadOnDisk(
      server,
      home,
      projects,
      reprojecting,
      fetchReported(changes).pipe(Effect.as(closedLoomChanges(server, changes))),
      () => [],
    ),
  )
  server.documents.onDidClose(({ document }) => {
    const uri = URI.parse(document.uri)
    runtime.runSync(forgetSeen(seen, uri))
    rereadOnDisk(
      server,
      home,
      projects,
      reprojecting,
      isLoom(uri) ? closeOf(uri, document.getText()) : Effect.succeed([]),
      (changes) => (changes.length > 0 ? [uri] : []),
    )
  })
}

## An edit reaches the other open chapters

A chapter the editor has open changes on every keystroke, and other open chapters read it. One open chapter draws a section of another in by an anchor, or imports a file the other tangles. A rename in the editor edits both at once: the section's title in one chapter, and the anchor that names it in the other. Until the edits are saved, the text the editor holds is the only place the new names exist.

The compiler builds a chapter from the editor's text only when the editor asks about that chapter. Volar asks about the chapter a request names, and about the chapters it draws in by anchors it has already resolved. So a chapter the person is not looking at can keep the build of its text from before an edit. Another chapter that reads it then reads the old text. After a rename across two chapters, the chapter with the anchor names a section its neighbour no longer holds, and the server reports the anchor as unresolved until the neighbour is asked about too.

So the server rebuilds every edited chapter before it answers any request. `watchLooms` notes each loom the editor opens or changes in the `edited` set, through `recordEdit`. Noting an edit is all a keystroke costs. The rebuilding waits for the next request, so a burst of keystrokes costs one rebuild per chapter.

`openSource` reads a loom the way the editor shows it. It reads an open loom's text from the editor and any other loom through the `DocumentSource` service. `rebuildEdited` takes the `edited` set, leaving it empty, and rebuilds each loom in it from `openSource` through the compiler's `rebuild`. It keeps a change for each loom whose face changed, the part of a module other files can see. A rebuild that fails logs its cause and counts as a change. The set is already empty by then, so a failure must not end the pass: the other looms in it still rebuild, and the request is still answered.

=>

const openSource = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  documents: DocumentSource['Service'],
  source: WorkspaceSource['Service'],
): Source => ({
  read: (path) =>
    Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(server.documents.get(source.uriOf(path))), {
      onSome: (document) => Effect.succeed(document.getText()),
      onNone: () => documents.read(path),
    }),
  list: Option.none(),
})

const recordEdit = (
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
  uri: URI,
): Effect.Effect<void, never, WorkspaceSource> =>
  isLoom(uri)
    ? WorkspaceSource.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((source) =>
          Ref.update(edited, (paths) => new Set([...paths, source.locationOf(uri)])),
        ),
      )
    : Effect.void

const rebuildEdited = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>, never, LoomCompiler | DocumentSource | WorkspaceSource> =>
  Effect.all([
    Ref.getAndSet(edited, new Set<string>()),
    LoomCompiler,
    DocumentSource,
    WorkspaceSource,
  ]).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(([paths, compiler, documents, source]) =>
      Effect.forEach(paths, (path) =>
        compiler.rebuild(openSource(server, documents, source), path).pipe(
          Effect.catchCause((cause) =>
            Effect.logError(`loom: rebuilding ${path} failed`, cause).pipe(Effect.as(true)),
          ),
          Effect.map((changed) =>
            changed
              ? Option.some<FileEvent>({
                  uri: source.uriOf(path).toString(),
                  type: FileChangeType.Changed,
                })
              : Option.none(),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
    Effect.map(Array.getSomes),
  )

~

An edit whose face changed reaches the open chapters of its workspace the way a change on disk does. `reproject` marks them stale and asks Volar to refresh. An edit only to prose leaves the face as it was, and reaches nothing. The other open chapters keep their projections, and Volar keeps the diagnostics it cached for them.

`settleEdits` runs both before the project the server hands to Volar answers a request, while it holds the `reprojecting` semaphore. The rebuilding runs with Effect's `PreventSchedulerYield` set, so its fiber never yields between steps. Every step of a build is synchronous, so the rebuilding finishes within one turn of the event loop. A fiber that paused in the middle of a build would hold the build cache's lock, and a synchronous build in one of Volar's hooks would fail on it. Only the marking waits on a promise, and the marking never touches the build cache.

=>

const settleEdits = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): Effect.Effect<void, never, LoomCompiler | DocumentSource | WorkspaceSource> =>
  rebuildEdited(server, edited).pipe(
    Effect.provideService(Scheduler.PreventSchedulerYield, true),
    Effect.flatMap((changes) => reproject(server, home, projects, changes, [])),
  )

## A box kept in step as the author types

A box that points to a loom holds a copy of the loom's summary, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" describes: a chapter box copies its chapter's opening prose, and a discussion box copies its discussion's summary. The server keeps each copy in step with its source while the author types, in any editor. An edit to a loom's summary reaches every copy, and an edit to a chapter box's copy reaches the chapter. The compiler's `summaryEdits` decides what to carry across. It carries a change only between a box and a loom that were in step before it, so a copy that was already out of date stays a warning.

`keepInStep` follows each change of a loom. It remembers in `seen` the text the editor held for each open loom, so every change comes with the text before it. The first text it sees for a loom, when the editor opens it, is only remembered. A later change rebuilds the edited chapters first, so the corpus holds what the editor shows, and then asks `summaryEdits` for the edits. It holds the `reprojecting` semaphore while it reads the corpus, and lets it go before it asks the editor to apply anything.

`applySummaryEdits` sends the edits to the editor as one workspace edit, with one `TextDocumentEdit` for each loom they change. Each names the version at which the editor holds that loom, so an editor that has changed the loom since refuses an edit placed in its older text. The editor applies the edit as a change to the other loom, which reaches `keepInStep` in turn. By then the box and its chapter match, so nothing travels back.

=>

const applySummaryEdits = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  edits: ReadonlyArray<SummaryEdit>,
): Effect.Effect<void, never, WorkspaceSource> =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(edits)
    ? WorkspaceSource.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((source) =>
          Effect.promise(() =>
            server.connection.workspace.applyEdit({
              label: 'Keep a box in step',
              edit: {
                documentChanges: Array.map(
                  Object.entries(Array.groupBy(edits, (edit) => edit.path)),
                  ([path, inLoom]) => ({
                    textDocument: {
                      uri: source.uriOf(path).toString(),
                      version: server.documents.get(source.uriOf(path))?.version ?? null,
                    },
                    edits: Array.map(inLoom, (edit) => ({ range: edit.range, newText: edit.text })),
                  }),
                ),
              },
            }),
          ),
        ),
        Effect.asVoid,
      )
    : Effect.void

const keepInStep = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  reprojecting: Semaphore.Semaphore,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
  seen: Ref.Ref<ReadonlyMap<string, string>>,
  uri: URI,
  after: string,
): void => {
  if (!isLoom(uri)) return
  void runtime.runFork(
    Ref.modify(
      seen,
      (texts) =>
        [
          Option.fromNullishOr(texts.get(uri.toString())),
          new Map(texts).set(uri.toString(), after),
        ] as const,
    ).pipe(
      Effect.flatMap((before) =>
        Option.match(
          Option.filter(before, (was) => was !== after),
          {
            onNone: () => Effect.void,
            onSome: (was) =>
              reprojecting
                .withPermits(1)(
                  settleEdits(server, home, projects, edited).pipe(
                    Effect.andThen(Effect.all([LoomCompiler, WorkspaceSource])),
                    Effect.flatMap(([compiler, source]) =>
                      compiler.summaryEdits(source.locationOf(uri), was, after),
                    ),
                  ),
                )
                .pipe(Effect.flatMap((edits) => applySummaryEdits(server, edits))),
          },
        ),
      ),
      Effect.catchCause((cause) =>
        Effect.logError(`loom: keeping ${uri.toString()} in step failed`, cause),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

const forgetSeen = (seen: Ref.Ref<ReadonlyMap<string, string>>, uri: URI): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Ref.update(seen, (texts) => new Map(Array.filter([...texts], ([key]) => key !== uri.toString())))

## The program

A `ManagedRuntime`, built once from the pipeline layers, warms the services the server runs. It builds them over the `Environment` layer of the chapter "Where a workspace is kept", which picks the file system or remote storage from the `LOOM_STORAGE_URL` variable. It also holds the `LanguagePackages` the compiler asks to write a reference's path. The editor projects a loom inside Volar's synchronous hook, where nothing can wait for a package to load, so the runtime takes the `held` layer, which loads nothing. `loomServicePlugins` hands it each workspace's packages as it loads them, before any loom of that workspace is projected. A configuration that fails, such as a URL with no key, becomes a defect, so the runtime itself never fails and the server reports the defect as the editor connects. The language plugin runs the per-keystroke projection on it, and resolves each file's activated languages from `LoomConfig` on it too. A format request runs `LoomFormatter` on it.

The `onInitialize` handler runs one Effect on that runtime. The program reads the workspace folder the editor opened, falling back to the working directory when the editor names none. `homeOf` turns the folder's address into a location through the `WorkspaceSource` service, and asks the source for the workspace that location belongs to. Remote storage names its one workspace whatever folder the editor opened, or when it opened none. It takes `home` to be the workspace that folder belongs to. It opens `home` through `openWorkspace`, which asks the `WorkspaceSource` service for the workspace and hands every loom's text to the `DocumentSource` service to hold, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes. Only a folder with a manifest is opened, so a folder outside every workspace holds nothing, and its looms are read from the disk as they are asked for. A failure to read the manifest fails the `initialize` request, so the editor hears of a wrong storage key as it connects. A failure to open a workspace whose manifest arrived only logs, since every read falls back to the source. The program then builds the `TypescriptSdk` with `typescriptSdkFor`, from the options the editor sent, and loads no compiler while it does.

The program then makes two caches, each keyed by a workspace's root, the `reprojecting` semaphore, whose one permit a reprojection holds while it marks the open looms stale, and the `edited` set of looms the editor has changed since the last request. `services` holds the plugins `loomServicePlugins` collects for a workspace. `projects` holds the project `projectOver` builds over those plugins. A cache fills a workspace's entry the first time a request needs it, and fills it once, however many requests ask at the same moment. The program collects the plugins of `home` right away, because Volar announces the server's capabilities from them. The handler registers `workspaceProject`, built over both caches, the semaphore, and the set, and those plugins with Volar.

A chapter the editor opens can be answered only once the corpus around it is built, and building it is the first and longest wait after the server starts. So the server starts building it before the editor asks. Once the handler has its answer for `initialize`, it schedules `warmCorpus` for `home` a few milliseconds later, so the answer goes out first.

The warm-up runs in one synchronous piece, with `runSync`, never as a background fiber. Volar's hooks run the compiler synchronously too, and a synchronous run cannot wait. A background fiber can pause in the middle of building a module while it holds the build cache's lock. A chapter the editor opened in that moment would find the lock held, and its synchronous build would fail instead of waiting. Run in one piece, the warm-up finishes before the server reads the editor's next message, so a chapter opened meanwhile waits in line and then finds its corpus built. `warmCorpus` takes the first loom of the workspace `openWorkspace` opened and asks the compiler for that loom's reach, which builds the whole corpus into the build cache. When the person opens a chapter a moment later, its corpus is already built, or partly built, and the editor waits only for the rest. A folder that opened no workspace, or a workspace with no loom in its corpus, warms nothing, and a failure only logs, since the editor builds the corpus on demand anyway.

=>

const runtime = ManagedRuntime.make(
  Layer.mergeAll(LoomCompiler.layer, LoomFormatter.layer).pipe(
    Layer.provideMerge(LanguagePackages.held),
    Layer.provideMerge(DocumentSource.layer),
    Layer.provideMerge(Layer.orDie(Environment)),
    Layer.provideMerge(LoomConfig.layer),
  ),
)

const openWorkspace = (
  home: string,
): Effect.Effect<
  Option.Option<Workspace>,
  ReadError,
  WorkspaceSettings | WorkspaceSource | DocumentSource
> =>
  WorkspaceSettings.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((settings) => settings.manifest(home)),
    Effect.flatMap(
      Option.match({
        onNone: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none<Workspace>()),
        onSome: () =>
          Effect.gen(function* () {
            const source = yield* WorkspaceSource
            const documents = yield* DocumentSource
            const workspace = yield* source.open(home)
            yield* documents.hold(workspace.looms)
            return Option.some(workspace)
          }).pipe(
            Effect.catchCause((cause) =>
              Effect.logWarning('loom: could not open the workspace', cause).pipe(
                Effect.as(Option.none<Workspace>()),
              ),
            ),
          ),
      }),
    ),
  )

const warmCorpus = (workspace: Option.Option<Workspace>): Effect.Effect<void, never, LoomCompiler> =>
  pipe(
    Option.flatMap(workspace, (opened) => Array.head(Array.fromIterable(opened.looms.keys()))),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => Effect.void,
      onSome: (entry) =>
        LoomCompiler.pipe(Effect.flatMap((compiler) => Effect.asVoid(compiler.reach(entry)))),
    }),
    Effect.catchCause((cause) => Effect.logWarning('loom: could not warm the corpus', cause)),
  )

const homeOf = (folder: string | undefined): Effect.Effect<string, never, WorkspaceSource> =>
  WorkspaceSource.pipe(
    Effect.map((source) =>
      source.workspaceOf(
        Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(folder), {
          onNone: () => process.cwd(),
          onSome: (uri) => source.locationOf(URI.parse(uri)),
        }),
      ),
    ),
  )

export const startLanguageServer = (): void => {
  const connection = createConnection()
  const server = createServer(connection)

  connection.onInitialize(async (params) => {
    const home = await runtime.runPromise(homeOf(params.workspaceFolders?.[0]?.uri))
    const { workspace, services, projects, plugins, reprojecting, edited } = await runtime.runPromise(
      Effect.gen(function* () {
        const workspace = yield* openWorkspace(home)
        const sdk = yield* typescriptSdkFor(params.initializationOptions)
        const services = yield* Cache.make({
          capacity: Infinity,
          lookup: (workspace: string) => loomServicePlugins(runtime, sdk, workspace),
        })
        const projects = yield* Cache.make({
          capacity: Infinity,
          lookup: (workspace: string) =>
            Cache.get(services, workspace).pipe(
              Effect.flatMap((plugins) => Effect.sync(() => projectOver(server, plugins))),
            ),
        })
        const plugins = yield* Cache.get(services, home)
        const reprojecting = yield* Semaphore.make(1)
        const edited = yield* Ref.make<ReadonlySet<string>>(new Set())
        return { workspace, services, projects, plugins, reprojecting, edited }
      }),
    )
    setTimeout(() => runtime.runSync(warmCorpus(workspace)), 10)
    return server.initialize(
      params,
      workspaceProject(server, home, services, projects, reprojecting, edited),
      [...plugins],
    )
  })
  connection.onInitialized(() => server.initialized())
  connection.onShutdown(server.shutdown)
  connection.listen()
}

## The server module [src/LoomServer.ts]

The server module assembles the imports, the compilers a project may ask for, the projects kept per workspace, the rereading of closed chapters, the boxes kept in step, and the program, and exports `startLanguageServer`. It also exports `classicTypeScript`, `projectOver`, `workspaceProject`, `refreshProject`, `rebuildUnder`, and `closeOf`, so the cases can drive each one directly. It runs nothing on import — a caller decides when to start the server, so the same module serves both an editor that spawns it and the `loom lsp` command that calls it in-process.

=>

::[Imports]

::[The compilers a project may ask for]

::[Every loom answers to its own workspace]

::[A chapter changes behind the editor's back]

::[An edit reaches the other open chapters]

::[A box kept in step as the author types]

::[The program]

## The run-on-import entry [src/index.ts]

This thin entry starts the server the moment it loads. The VS Code extension bundles it as `server.js`, and the `loom-language-server` bin runs it — both expect loading the module to start the server.

=>

import { startLanguageServer } from './LoomServer'

startLanguageServer()

## Testing a nested workspace

The cases build two workspaces, one nested in the other, the way a Git worktree sits inside the repository it was checked out from. A workspace's store holds a stand-in for the TypeScript service rather than the real one, so the cases need no build and no network. The outer stand-in offers no `format`. The nested one formats by squeezing every double space to one. Both workspaces hold the same loom, whose code is spaced wide.

A stand-in reads Effect from the runtime the host lends, under `runtimeKey`, as a published service does. It declares the version `serviceVersion`, so the loader loads it without a warning. `install` puts a stand-in in a workspace's store, `configure` writes the workspace's `config.yaml`, and `loomAt` writes its loom. `workspaceAt` does all three, and it fills the store before it writes the configuration, in the order `loom add` follows.

=>

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const loomOf = (code: string): string =>
  lines('---', 'Language: TypeScript', '---', '', '# Answer', '', '=>', '', code)

const standIn = (formats: boolean): string =>
  lines(
    `const { Effect } = globalThis.${runtimeKey}.modules.effect`,
    'export default {',
    "  id: 'typescript',",
    "  displayName: 'TypeScript',",
    "  extensions: ['.ts'],",
    '  plugins: () => Effect.succeed([]),',
    ...(formats
      ? ["  format: (request) => Effect.succeed(request.code.replaceAll('  ', ' ')),"]
      : []),
    '}',
  )

const activated = lines('languages:', '  typescript: {}')

const inactive = lines('languages: {}')

const install = (root: string, service: string): void => {
  const store = join(root, '.loom', 'services', 'node_modules', servicePackage('typescript'))
  mkdirSync(store, { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(
    join(store, 'package.json'),
    JSON.stringify({
      name: servicePackage('typescript'),
      version: serviceVersion,
      type: 'module',
      main: 'index.js',
    }),
  )
  writeFileSync(join(store, 'index.js'), service)
}

const configure = (root: string, config: string): void => {
  mkdirSync(join(root, '.loom'), { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(join(root, '.loom', 'config.yaml'), config)
}

const loomAt = (root: string): string => {
  mkdirSync(join(root, 'corpus'), { recursive: true })
  const loom = join(root, 'corpus', 'answer.loom')
  writeFileSync(loom, loomOf('const  answer  =  42'))
  return loom
}

const workspaceAt = (root: string, service: string): string => {
  install(root, service)
  configure(root, activated)
  return loomAt(root)
}

~

The cases start the server from source the way an editor does. The server runs as a process of its own and speaks the Language Server Protocol over its standard input and output. The client hands it the TypeScript this repository installs, as VS Code hands over its own. It leaves `LOOM_HOME` out of the server's environment, since that variable would point every workspace at one store.

The client declares that it watches files for the server, as an editor does. It does not watch any itself. A case reports a file created, changed, or deleted through `changed`, the way an editor's watcher would, and a change is the default. The client answers every request the server makes of it with nothing, whether the server asks for the editor's settings or asks it to watch a file. It keeps each workspace edit the server asks it to apply, and `applied` waits up to ten seconds for the first one, so a case can read the edits the server sent without applying them. `open` shows the server a loom, `edit` replaces the text the editor holds for it without saving, under a version that only grows, `shut` closes it without saving, `format` asks for the edits that format it, and `texts` reads the text out of those edits. `diagnostics` asks for the problems the server finds in a loom and returns their messages. `fixes` asks for the code actions the server offers on one line of a loom, `hints` asks for the inlay hints it shows across a whole loom, `folds` asks for the ranges it folds and the text each shows folded, and `completions` asks for what it offers at one place in a loom, keeping each offer's label and edit.

=>

const serverEntry = fileURLToPath(new URL('../src/index.ts', import.meta.url))

const tsdk = dirname(createRequire(import.meta.url).resolve('typescript/lib/typescript.js'))

const editorAt = async (folder: string) => {
  const env = Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(process.env).filter(([key]) => key !== 'LOOM_HOME'))
  const child = spawn(process.execPath, [serverEntry, '--stdio'], {
    env,
    stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'ignore'],
  })
  const connection = createMessageConnection(
    new StreamMessageReader(child.stdout),
    new StreamMessageWriter(child.stdin),
  )
  const asked: Array<ApplyWorkspaceEditParams> = []
  connection.onRequest((method, params) => {
    if (method === 'workspace/applyEdit') asked.push(params as ApplyWorkspaceEditParams)
    return null
  })
  const applied = (wait = 10_000): Promise<ReadonlyArray<ApplyWorkspaceEditParams>> =>
    asked.length > 0 || wait <= 0
      ? Promise.resolve([...asked])
      : new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(applied(wait - 50)), 50))
  connection.listen()
  await connection.sendRequest('initialize', {
    processId: process.pid,
    rootUri: pathToFileURL(folder).href,
    workspaceFolders: [{ uri: pathToFileURL(folder).href, name: 'outer' }],
    capabilities: { workspace: { didChangeWatchedFiles: { dynamicRegistration: true } } },
    initializationOptions: { typescript: { tsdk } },
  })
  await connection.sendNotification('initialized', {})
  const open = (path: string): Promise<void> =>
    connection.sendNotification('textDocument/didOpen', {
      textDocument: {
        uri: pathToFileURL(path).href,
        languageId: 'loom',
        version: 1,
        text: readFileSync(path, 'utf8'),
      },
    })
  const edit = (path: string, text: string): Promise<void> =>
    connection.sendNotification('textDocument/didChange', {
      textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href, version: Date.now() },
      contentChanges: [{ text }],
    })
  const format = async (path: string): Promise<ReadonlyArray<TextEdit>> =>
    (await connection.sendRequest<TextEdit[] | null>('textDocument/formatting', {
      textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href },
      options: { tabSize: 2, insertSpaces: true },
    })) ?? []
  const diagnostics = async (path: string): Promise<ReadonlyArray<string>> =>
    Array.map(
      (
        await connection.sendRequest<{ readonly items: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> }>(
          'textDocument/diagnostic',
          { textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href } },
        )
      ).items,
      (diagnostic) => diagnostic.message,
    )
  const fixes = async (path: string, line: number): Promise<ReadonlyArray<CodeAction>> =>
    (await connection.sendRequest<CodeAction[] | null>('textDocument/codeAction', {
      textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href },
      range: { start: { line, character: 0 }, end: { line: line + 1, character: 0 } },
      context: { diagnostics: [] },
    })) ?? []
  const hints = async (
    path: string,
  ): Promise<ReadonlyArray<{ readonly position: unknown; readonly label: unknown }>> =>
    Array.map(
      (await connection.sendRequest<InlayHint[] | null>('textDocument/inlayHint', {
        textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href },
        range: { start: { line: 0, character: 0 }, end: { line: 10_000, character: 0 } },
      })) ?? [],
      (hint) => ({ position: hint.position, label: hint.label }),
    )
  const folds = async (path: string): Promise<ReadonlyArray<FoldingRange>> =>
    (await connection.sendRequest<FoldingRange[] | null>('textDocument/foldingRange', {
      textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href },
    })) ?? []
  const completions = async (
    path: string,
    line: number,
    character: number,
  ): Promise<ReadonlyArray<{ readonly label: unknown; readonly textEdit: unknown }>> =>
    Array.map(
      (
        (await connection.sendRequest<CompletionList | null>('textDocument/completion', {
          textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href },
          position: { line, character },
        })) ?? { items: [] }
      ).items,
      (item) => ({ label: item.label, textEdit: item.textEdit }),
    )
  const shut = (path: string): Promise<void> =>
    connection.sendNotification('textDocument/didClose', {
      textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href },
    })
  const changed = (path: string, type: FileChangeType = FileChangeType.Changed): Promise<void> =>
    connection.sendNotification('workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles', {
      changes: [{ uri: pathToFileURL(path).href, type }],
    })
  const close = (): void => {
    connection.dispose()
    child.kill()
  }
  return {
    open,
    edit,
    shut,
    format,
    diagnostics,
    fixes,
    hints,
    folds,
    completions,
    changed,
    applied,
    close,
  }
}

const texts = (edits: ReadonlyArray<TextEdit>): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Array.map(edits, (edit) => edit.newText)

~

In the first case the editor opens the outer folder, as it opens a repository, and asks the server to format each loom. The outer loom must come back unchanged, since its workspace offers no formatter for TypeScript. The nested loom must come back with its code squeezed, which only the nested workspace's service can do.

The second case activates a language while the server runs. The nested workspace starts with no language active and nothing in its store, so its loom formats to itself. The case then installs the formatting stand-in and activates TypeScript, as `loom add` would, and reports the changed `config.yaml`. The next format must squeeze the loom's code, with no restart in between.

=>

describe('LoomServer — a loom in a nested workspace', () => {
  it('is formatted by the services of its own workspace', async () => {
    const outer = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const outerLoom = workspaceAt(outer, standIn(false))
    const nestedLoom = workspaceAt(join(outer, 'nested'), standIn(true))
    const editor = await editorAt(outer)
    try {
      await editor.open(outerLoom)
      await editor.open(nestedLoom)
      expect(await editor.format(outerLoom)).toEqual([])
      expect(texts(await editor.format(nestedLoom))).toEqual([loomOf('const answer = 42')])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('is served by a language its workspace activates while the server runs', async () => {
    const outer = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    workspaceAt(outer, standIn(false))
    const nested = join(outer, 'nested')
    configure(nested, inactive)
    const nestedLoom = loomAt(nested)
    const editor = await editorAt(outer)
    try {
      await editor.open(nestedLoom)
      expect(await editor.format(nestedLoom)).toEqual([])
      install(nested, standIn(true))
      configure(nested, activated)
      await editor.changed(join(nested, '.loom', 'config.yaml'))
      expect(texts(await editor.format(nestedLoom))).toEqual([loomOf('const answer = 42')])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a chapter that changes on disk

A chapter can change while the editor has it closed, and a chapter the editor has open can depend on it. Each case writes its chapters into one workspace. `chapterAt` writes a chapter into the workspace's corpus, and `chapter` is the text of a chapter with one section, under a given title, holding a given line of code. An anchor in this loom's own code would be read as an anchor of this loom, so `anchor` spells one in two pieces.

=>

const chapterAt = (root: string, name: string, text: string): string => {
  mkdirSync(join(root, 'corpus'), { recursive: true })
  const path = join(root, 'corpus', name)
  writeFileSync(path, text)
  return path
}

const chapter = (title: string, code: string): string =>
  lines('---', 'Language: TypeScript', '---', '', `# ${title}`, '', '=>', '', code)

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

~

The first case checks Loom's own diagnostics. A closed chapter, `answer.loom`, holds the section `Answer`, and the open chapter `Main` draws it in with an anchor that names both the section and its chapter. The server finds nothing wrong with `Main`. The case then renames the section in the closed chapter on disk, as a `git checkout` might, and reports the change. `Main` now names a section no chapter holds, and the server must say so.

The next two cases change the set of chapters. In one, `Main` names a chapter that does not exist yet, and the case creates it. In the other, the case deletes the chapter `Main` draws from. Each reports the change. `Main`'s anchor must resolve once the chapter exists, and fail once it is gone.

The fourth case checks the cache Volar keeps for a service that reads no other file, the one the TypeScript service declares. Its workspace activates `checking`, a stand-in TypeScript service that declares the same. On every TypeScript section it reports one diagnostic, whose message is the code of every root the corpus composes, sorted and joined. Asked to count, it also appends how many diagnostics it has reported for that document so far, so a case can tell a fresh check from one Volar kept. It counts each document apart, so diagnostics Volar pushes for another chapter never move the count. A real type check reads another chapter's exports the same way, through the corpus composition.

Each of its three chapters tangles its one section to a file, since the composition holds only the roots that tangle somewhere. The case opens two of them, `main.loom` and `note.loom`, and gives `note.loom` an unsaved edit. A third chapter, `answer.loom`, stays closed. The case rewrites the closed chapter on disk and reports the change. The message on `Main` must now carry the new answer, which only a reprojection of `Main` can bring past Volar's cache. It must still carry the unsaved note, which the server loses if the reprojection rebuilds an open chapter from disk.

The last case closes the edited chapter without saving. The editor reports no change on disk, since none happened, yet the unsaved note now exists nowhere. The message on `Main` must carry the saved note again.

=>

const checking = (loom: string, counted = false): string =>
  lines(
    `const { Effect } = globalThis.${runtimeKey}.modules.effect`,
    'const reported = new Map()',
    `const { Composition } = globalThis.${runtimeKey}.modules['@athrio/loom-lang-services/LanguageService']`,
    'const count = (uri) => reported.set(uri, (reported.get(uri) ?? 0) + 1).get(uri)',
    'const composed = (composition) =>',
    `  composition.rootsFor(${JSON.stringify(loom)}).map((file) => file.content.trim()).sort().join(' | ')`,
    'export default {',
    "  id: 'typescript',",
    "  displayName: 'TypeScript',",
    "  extensions: ['.ts'],",
    '  plugins: () => Effect.gen(function* () {',
    '    const composition = yield* Composition',
    '    return [{',
    "      name: 'checking',",
    '      capabilities: { diagnosticProvider: { interFileDependencies: false, workspaceDiagnostics: false } },',
    '      create: () => ({',
    "        provideDiagnostics: (document) => document.languageId === 'typescript'",
    `          ? [{ range: { start: { line: 0, character: 0 }, end: { line: 0, character: 1 } }, message: composed(composition)${counted ? " + ' #' + count(document.uri)" : ''} }]`,
    '          : [],',
    '      }),',
    '    }]',
    '  }),',
    '}',
  )

describe('LoomServer — a chapter that changes on disk', () => {
  it('answers an open chapter from the text a closed one holds now', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const opened = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', anchor('Answer')))
    const closed = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(opened)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
      chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Question', 'const answer = 42'))
      await editor.changed(closed)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([
        `Unresolved anchor: no section named \`Answer\`.`,
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('resolves an anchor into a chapter created on disk', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const opened = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', anchor('Answer')))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(opened)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([
        `Unresolved anchor: no section named \`Answer\`.`,
      ])
      const created = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42'))
      await editor.changed(created, FileChangeType.Created)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('fails an anchor into a chapter deleted from disk', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const opened = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', anchor('Answer')))
    const deleted = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(opened)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
      rmSync(deleted)
      await editor.changed(deleted, FileChangeType.Deleted)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([
        `Unresolved anchor: no section named \`Answer\`.`,
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('checks an open chapter again, keeping the unsaved text of the others', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main))
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main [main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const closed = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer [answer.ts]', 'const answer = 42'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'unsaved'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(note)).toEqual([
        "const answer = 42 | const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'",
      ])
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual([
        "const answer = 42 | const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'",
      ])
      chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer [answer.ts]', 'const answer = 43'))
      await editor.changed(closed)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual([
        "const answer = 43 | const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'",
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('composes the saved text of a chapter closed without saving', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main))
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main [main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'unsaved'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(note)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
      await editor.shut(note)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'saved'"])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a box kept in step

The server keeps a chapter box and its chapter in step while the author types. `World` holds a box that points to the chapter `Rooms`, and the box's body matches the chapter's opening. The author edits the box, and the server asks the editor to write the box's new body into the chapter's opening, on line 2 of `Rooms`.

=>

describe('LoomServer — a chapter box kept in step', () => {
  it('writes an edit to the box into the opening of its chapter', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const box = (body: string) =>
      lines('# The world', '', 'The world holds rooms.', '', '::: Rooms {Chapter}', body, ':::')
    const world = chapterAt(root, 'world.loom', box('A room is a grid of cells.'))
    const rooms = chapterAt(root, 'rooms.loom', lines('# Rooms', '', 'A room is a grid of cells.'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(world)
      await editor.open(rooms)
      await editor.edit(world, box('A room is a grid.'))
      const [asked] = await editor.applied()
      expect(asked?.edit.documentChanges).toEqual([
        {
          textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(rooms).href, version: 1 },
          edits: [
            {
              range: { start: { line: 2, character: 0 }, end: { line: 2, character: 26 } },
              newText: 'A room is a grid.',
            },
          ],
        },
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing an edit that reaches other open chapters

An edit to one open chapter changes what the others read, before it is ever saved. These cases edit open chapters and never touch the disk.

The first case renames a section across two open chapters, the way a rename in the editor does. `Main` draws in the section `Answer` from `answer.loom`. The case renames the section to `Question` in the editor, and renames the anchor in `Main` to match, and saves neither. It then asks only about `Main`. `Main`'s anchor must resolve against the section as the editor holds it, not as the disk still has it.

The second case gives `note.loom` an unsaved edit to its code and asks only about `Main`. The message on `Main` must carry the edited note, which only a reprojection of `Main` can bring past Volar's cache.

The third case checks that an edit only to prose costs the other chapters nothing. Its stand-in counts the diagnostics it reports and appends the count to each message. An edit to the prose of `note.loom` changes nothing `Main` can see, so `Main` keeps the diagnostics Volar cached for it, and the count stays where it was. An edit to the code of `note.loom` then changes what `Main` reads, and `Main` is checked again.

=>

describe('LoomServer — an edit that reaches other open chapters', () => {
  it('resolves an anchor renamed across two open chapters before either is saved', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const opened = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', anchor('Answer')))
    const answer = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(opened)
      await editor.open(answer)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
      await editor.edit(answer, chapter('Question', 'const answer = 42'))
      await editor.edit(opened, chapter('Main', anchor('Question')))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('checks an open chapter against the unsaved code of another', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main))
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main [main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'saved'"])
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'unsaved'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('checks other open chapters again only when an edit changes what they read', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main, true))
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main [main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'saved' #1"])
      await editor.edit(
        note,
        chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'").replace(
          '[note.ts]\n',
          '[note.ts]\n\nA word of prose.\n',
        ),
      )
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'saved' #1"])
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'edited'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'edited' #2"])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a configuration that changes

A workspace's configuration feeds into what its looms compose. A value warp can read a variable the configuration defines, and the code that anchors the warp composes its value. So a change to a variable changes the composed code of every loom that reads it, with no loom changed at all.

The case defines the variable `answer` in the workspace's configuration. `Main` binds a value warp to it and composes the warp's value into its code. `note.loom` is open too, with an unsaved edit. The case changes the variable on disk and reports the changed configuration. The message on `Main` must carry the new value, and must still carry the unsaved note.

=>

const configuredWith = (answer: number): string =>
  lines('languages:', '  typescript: {}', 'variables:', `  answer: ${answer}`)

const readingAnswer = lines(
  '---',
  'Language: TypeScript',
  '---',
  '',
  '# Main [main.ts]',
  '',
  '{{ answer = ${answer} }}',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  `const main = ${anchor('answer')}`,
)

describe('LoomServer — a configuration that changes', () => {
  it('composes an open chapter under the new configuration, keeping unsaved text', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main))
    configure(root, configuredWith(42))
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', readingAnswer)
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note [note.ts]', "const note = 'unsaved'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 42 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
      configure(root, configuredWith(43))
      await editor.changed(join(root, '.loom', 'config.yaml'))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 43 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a compiler setting that changes

A `tsconfig.json` and a `package.json` decide a project's compiler and its options, and the TypeScript service reads both when it builds a project's program. So a change to either must give the workspace fresh services. The case's stand-in, `creating`, counts how many times a language service has created its plugin, and reports the count on every TypeScript section. The workspace's first services report one. The case writes a `tsconfig.json` and reports it created, and the next check must report two. It writes a `package.json`, and the next check must report three.

=>

const creating = lines(
  `const { Effect } = globalThis.${runtimeKey}.modules.effect`,
  'const created = { count: 0 }',
  'export default {',
  "  id: 'typescript',",
  "  displayName: 'TypeScript',",
  "  extensions: ['.ts'],",
  '  plugins: () => Effect.succeed([{',
  "    name: 'creating',",
  '    capabilities: { diagnosticProvider: { interFileDependencies: false, workspaceDiagnostics: false } },',
  '    create: () => {',
  '      created.count += 1',
  '      const mine = created.count',
  '      return {',
  "        provideDiagnostics: (document) => document.languageId === 'typescript'",
  "          ? [{ range: { start: { line: 0, character: 0 }, end: { line: 0, character: 1 } }, message: 'created ' + mine }]",
  '          : [],',
  '      }',
  '    },',
  '  }]),',
  '}',
)

describe('LoomServer — a compiler setting that changes', () => {
  it('builds the services afresh when a tsconfig.json or a package.json changes', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    install(root, creating)
    configure(root, activated)
    const main = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main [main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(['created 1'])
      writeFileSync(join(root, 'tsconfig.json'), '{}\n')
      await editor.changed(join(root, 'tsconfig.json'), FileChangeType.Created)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(['created 2'])
      writeFileSync(join(root, 'package.json'), '{}\n')
      await editor.changed(join(root, 'package.json'), FileChangeType.Created)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(['created 3'])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing where a TypeScript 6 comes from

`classicTypeScript` looks for a TypeScript 6 in the project first, then in the editor's own copy. The cases stand two fake copies in a scratch folder. Each is a `typescript.js` that exports only the place it stands for. `editorCopy` writes one where an editor would point the server, and `projectAt` writes a project whose `node_modules` holds the other.

The project's copy wins although the editor names its own. A folder with no TypeScript of its own gets the editor's copy. A second request for the same project hands back the very module the first loaded, since the server loads each compiler once.

=>

const fakeTypeScript = (folder: string, from: string): void => {
  mkdirSync(folder, { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(join(folder, 'typescript.js'), `module.exports = { from: ${JSON.stringify(from)} }\n`)
}

const editorCopy = (root: string): string => {
  fakeTypeScript(join(root, 'editor', 'lib'), 'editor')
  return join(root, 'editor', 'lib')
}

const projectAt = (root: string): string => {
  const typescript = join(root, 'project', 'node_modules', 'typescript')
  fakeTypeScript(join(typescript, 'lib'), 'project')
  writeFileSync(join(typescript, 'package.json'), JSON.stringify({ name: 'typescript', version: '6.0.0' }))
  return join(root, 'project')
}

describe('LoomServer — where a TypeScript 6 comes from', () => {
  it('prefers the project\'s copy, then the editor\'s, and loads each once', () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-classic-')))
    const tsdk = editorCopy(root)
    const project = projectAt(root)
    const bare = join(root, 'bare')
    mkdirSync(bare)
    const fromOf = (result: ReturnType<ReturnType<typeof classicTypeScript>>): unknown =>
      Result.isSuccess(result) ? (result.success as unknown as { from: string }).from : result.failure
    const withEditor = classicTypeScript({ typescript: { tsdk } })
    expect(fromOf(withEditor(project))).toBe('project')
    expect(fromOf(withEditor(bare))).toBe('editor')
    const first = withEditor(project)
    const again = withEditor(project)
    expect(Result.isSuccess(first) && Result.isSuccess(again) && first.success === again.success).toBe(true)
  })
})

## Testing a fix for an ambiguous anchor

An ambiguous anchor offers the editor a fix for each path that names one of its candidates. The two chapters called `Setup` open two parts, `Saying things` and `Doing things`, and `Main` names `Setup` bare on line 8, its name starting at character 3. The server offers two quick fixes on that line, one for each part, and each writes its path over the anchor's name in `main.loom`.

=>

describe('LoomServer — a fix for an ambiguous anchor', () => {
  it('offers the path through each part as a quick fix', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const setup = (part: string, code: string): string =>
      lines(
        '---',
        part,
        'Chapter 1: Setup',
        'Language: TypeScript',
        '---',
        '',
        '# Setup',
        '',
        '=>',
        '',
        code,
      )
    chapterAt(root, 'setup-one.loom', setup('Part I: Saying things', 'const one = 1'))
    chapterAt(root, 'setup-two.loom', setup('Part II: Doing things', 'const two = 2'))
    const main = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', anchor('Setup')))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      const actions = await editor.fixes(main, 8)
      expect(
        actions
          .map((action) => [
            action.title,
            action.kind,
            Object.values(action.edit?.changes ?? {})
              .flat()
              .map((edit) => [edit.newText, edit.range.start.character, edit.range.end.character]),
          ])
          .sort(),
      ).toEqual([
        ['Write the path `Doing things, Setup`', 'quickfix', [['Doing things, Setup', 3, 8]]],
        ['Write the path `Saying things, Setup`', 'quickfix', [['Saying things, Setup', 3, 8]]],
      ])
      expect(
        actions.every((action) =>
          Object.keys(action.edit?.changes ?? {}).every((uri) => uri.endsWith('/main.loom')),
        ),
      ).toBe(true)
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing the title beside a number

The editor shows the title a numbered reference stands for. `Main` names the section `Saying hello` through `Chapter 2`, on line 8, its name starting at character 3. The server answers an inlay hint request with the title `Words`, just after the number, at character 12.

=>

describe('LoomServer — the title beside a number', () => {
  it('shows the chapter title after a chapter named by number', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    chapterAt(
      root,
      'words.loom',
      lines(
        '---',
        'Part I, Chapter 2: Words',
        'Language: TypeScript',
        '---',
        '',
        '# Words',
        '',
        '## Saying hello',
        '',
        '=>',
        '',
        'const hi = 1',
      ),
    )
    const main = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', anchor('Chapter 2, Saying hello')))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      expect(await editor.hints(main)).toEqual([
        { position: { line: 8, character: 12 }, label: 'Words' },
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a box folded under its caption

The editor folds a box away under its caption. `Grid` holds a note named `Why a grid`, which opens on line 4 and closes on line 6. The server answers a folding request with a range over those lines, which shows the note's name when folded.

=>

describe('LoomServer — a box folded under its caption', () => {
  it('folds a box from its opening line to its closing line', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const grid = chapterAt(
      root,
      'grid.loom',
      lines(
        '# Grid',
        '',
        'The room is small.',
        '',
        '::: Why a grid',
        'The room needs no engine.',
        ':::',
      ),
    )
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(grid)
      expect(await editor.folds(grid)).toContainEqual({
        startLine: 4,
        endLine: 6,
        kind: 'region',
        collapsedText: 'Why a grid',
      })
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a label as it is typed

The server offers the labels an anchor takes while the author types between the anchor's braces. `Main` anchors `Greeting` on line 8, and the author has typed `To b` between the braces, from character 13 to character 17. Asked at the end of those four characters, the server offers both kinds an anchor takes, `To be settled` and `Reference`, each of which replaces them.

=>

describe('LoomServer — a label as it is typed', () => {
  it('offers the label an anchor takes between its braces', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const main = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', `${anchor('Greeting')}{To b}`))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      expect(await editor.completions(main, 8, 17)).toEqual([
        {
          label: 'To be settled',
          textEdit: {
            range: { start: { line: 8, character: 13 }, end: { line: 8, character: 17 } },
            newText: 'To be settled',
          },
        },
        {
          label: 'Reference',
          textEdit: {
            range: { start: { line: 8, character: 13 }, end: { line: 8, character: 17 } },
            newText: 'Reference',
          },
        },
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing the names a section must provide

An anchor that says which names its section provides is checked against what the section's language reports. The workspace activates `declaring`, a stand-in TypeScript service that reports a symbol for every `const` it finds in a TypeScript document. `Main` expects `greet` and `farewell` of the chapter `Greeting` in another loom, which declares only `greet`. `Local` expects `help` and `assist` of a section in its own loom, which declares only `help`. The server reports the one missing name in each, and only those.

=>

const declaring = lines(
  `const { Effect } = globalThis.${runtimeKey}.modules.effect`,
  'export default {',
  "  id: 'typescript',",
  "  displayName: 'TypeScript',",
  "  extensions: ['.ts'],",
  '  plugins: () => Effect.succeed([{',
  "    name: 'declaring',",
  '    capabilities: { documentSymbolProvider: true },',
  '    create: () => ({',
  '      provideDocumentSymbols: (document) =>',
  "        document.languageId !== 'typescript'",
  '          ? undefined',
  '          : [...document.getText().matchAll(/const (\\w+)/g)].map((match) => {',
  '              const start = document.positionAt(match.index + 6)',
  '              const end = document.positionAt(match.index + match[0].length)',
  '              return { name: match[1], kind: 13, range: { start, end }, selectionRange: { start, end } }',
  '            }),',
  '    }),',
  '  }]),',
  '}',
)

describe('LoomServer — names a section must provide', () => {
  it('reports each name an anchor expects that its section does not declare', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    install(root, declaring)
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(
      root,
      'greeting.loom',
      lines(
        '---',
        'Part I, Chapter 2: Greeting',
        'Language: TypeScript',
        '---',
        '',
        '# Greeting',
        '',
        '=>',
        '',
        'const greet = (name: string) => name',
      ),
    )
    const main = chapterAt(
      root,
      'main.loom',
      chapter('Main', `${anchor('Greeting')}{provides=[greet, farewell]}`),
    )
    const local = chapterAt(
      root,
      'local.loom',
      lines(
        '---',
        'Language: TypeScript',
        '---',
        '',
        '# Local',
        '',
        '=>',
        '',
        `${anchor('Helper')}{provides=[help, assist]}`,
        '',
        '## Helper',
        '',
        '=>',
        '',
        'const help = 1',
      ),
    )
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(local)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual([
        'Missing symbol: `Greeting` declares no `farewell`, which this anchor says it provides.',
      ])
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(local)).toEqual([
        'Missing symbol: `Helper` declares no `assist`, which this anchor says it provides.',
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a name that waits

An anchor may name a section or a chapter before anyone has written it, and its fault clears the moment the name appears, with no edit to the anchor's own loom. An edit that declares a title changes what other looms see of the loom it edits, so the server checks every open loom of the workspace again.

In the first case `Main` names `Words, Saying hello` while `words.loom` holds no such section. An unsaved edit adds it, and `Main` reports nothing. In the second, `Other` names a chapter, `A chapter to come`, that no loom declares. A loom that declares it, with code in its opening section, appears on disk, and `Other` reports nothing.

=>

describe('LoomServer — a name that waits', () => {
  it('clears when an edit declares the section or the chapter it names', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const wordsHead = [
      '---',
      'Part I, Chapter 2: Words',
      'Language: TypeScript',
      '---',
      '',
      '# Words',
    ]
    const words = chapterAt(root, 'words.loom', lines(...wordsHead))
    const main = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', anchor('Words, Saying hello')))
    const other = chapterAt(root, 'other.loom', chapter('Other', anchor('A chapter to come')))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(words)
      await editor.open(other)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual([
        'Unresolved anchor: no section named `Words, Saying hello`.',
      ])
      await editor.edit(
        words,
        lines(...wordsHead, '', '## Saying hello', '', '=>', '', 'const hi = 1'),
      )
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual([])
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(other)).toEqual([
        'Unresolved anchor: no section named `A chapter to come`.',
      ])
      const later = chapterAt(
        root,
        'later.loom',
        lines(
          '---',
          'Part I, Chapter 3: A chapter to come',
          'Language: TypeScript',
          '---',
          '',
          '# A chapter to come',
          '',
          '=>',
          '',
          'const later = 1',
        ),
      )
      await editor.changed(later, FileChangeType.Created)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(other)).toEqual([])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

~

The fixes for a missing section reach the editor as quick fixes. `Main` names `Saying goodbye`, which nothing declares, on line 8. One fix writes the heading `# Saying goodbye` at the end of `main.loom`, which is line 9 at character 0. The other writes `{To be settled}` just past the anchor, at character 18 of line 8. The editor may list the two in either order, so the case sorts them by title.

=>

  it('offers quick fixes that declare the missing section or mark it to be settled', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const main = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', anchor('Saying goodbye')))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      const actions = await editor.fixes(main, 8)
      expect(
        actions.map((action) => [
          action.title,
          Object.values(action.edit?.changes ?? {})
            .flat()
            .map((edit) => [edit.newText, edit.range.start.line, edit.range.start.character]),
        ]).sort(),
      ).toEqual([
        ['Declare the section `Saying goodbye`', [['\n# Saying goodbye\n', 9, 0]]],
        ['Mark `Saying goodbye` to be settled', [['{To be settled}', 8, 18]]],
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing the pass piece by piece

Some of what the pass does leaves no trace an editor can see. Volar holds a script in a language service, or holds it nowhere, and no request reveals which. A request that waits on the `reprojecting` semaphore gets the same answer it would have got without waiting. A close that rereads nothing looks the same as one that rereads a file with the same text. So these cases drive the server's parts directly, over stand-ins for Volar's objects.

`recording` is a stand-in for one language service. It holds every script anyone reads from it, and lists every script anyone deletes from it. `projectOf` is a stand-in project that answers every loom from one language service and holds two. `serverWith` is a stand-in server whose editor has the given looms open. The looms sit under a folder in no workspace, so each belongs to `home`. `closedEditor` is a stand-in server whose editor has nothing open, with the few parts a language service reads from a server.

=>

const recording = () => {
  const scripts = new Map<string, { isAssociationDirty?: boolean }>()
  const deleted: Array<string> = []
  const language = {
    scripts: {
      get: (uri: URI) => {
        const script = scripts.get(uri.toString()) ?? {}
        scripts.set(uri.toString(), script)
        return script
      },
      delete: (uri: URI) => {
        deleted.push(uri.toString())
      },
    },
  }
  return { languageService: { context: { language } }, scripts, deleted }
}

const projectOf = (
  answering: ReturnType<typeof recording>,
  other: ReturnType<typeof recording>,
): LanguageServerProject =>
  ({
    setup: () => {},
    reload: () => {},
    getLanguageService: async () => answering.languageService,
    getExistingLanguageServices: async () => [
      answering.languageService,
      other.languageService,
    ],
  }) as unknown as LanguageServerProject

const serverWith = (...open: ReadonlyArray<URI>): LanguageServer =>
  ({
    documents: { all: () => Array.map(open, (uri) => ({ uri: uri.toString() })) },
  }) as unknown as LanguageServer

const closedEditor = {
  documents: { all: () => [], get: () => undefined },
  workspaceFolders: { all: [] },
  configurations: { get: async () => undefined, onDidChange: () => ({ dispose: () => {} }) },
  fileWatcher: { onDidChangeWatchedFiles: () => ({ dispose: () => {} }) },
} as unknown as LanguageServer

const home = '/nowhere'

const mainUri = URI.file('/nowhere/corpus/main.loom')

const goneUri = URI.file('/nowhere/corpus/gone.loom')

~

The first case refreshes a project that holds two language services, the way a project with a `tsconfig.json` does. The editor has `main.loom` open, and the batch deleted `gone.loom`. The open loom must be marked stale in the language service that answers it, and left unread in the other, which would otherwise build a second projection of it. The deleted loom must be forgotten in both, since either may hold its script.

The second case closes looms three ways. It runs `closeOf` over the two sources the server runs it over, the `FileSystemSource` layer and a `DocumentSource` of its own. A loom whose text matches the file on disk amounts to no change. A loom whose text differs amounts to a change, and a loom whose file is gone amounts to a deletion.

The third case changes a workspace's configuration while the editor has looms open in it, in a workspace nested inside it, and in a folder outside both. Both workspaces exist on disk, each with its own configuration, so the nested one is a workspace of its own. `rebuildUnder` must note as edited every open loom the eviction reaches, the nested workspace's included, and none outside. A case over the real server could not show this. Volar pushes diagnostics for every open loom a moment after each change, and a push rebuilds a loom from the editor's text whatever `rebuildUnder` noted.

The fourth case holds the `reprojecting` semaphore, as a reprojection does, and asks the server's project for a loom's language service. The answer must wait until the semaphore is released.

The fifth case builds a workspace's project over an editor that has nothing open. It asks the project's language for a loom that sits on disk, as Volar does when a definition lands in a chapter the editor has closed. The project must project the loom from its saved text, so the answer can map back to its place there. Asked again, it must hand back the same snapshot, since a new snapshot would project the loom anew.

=>

describe('LoomServer — the pass piece by piece', () => {
  it('marks an open loom where it is answered and forgets a deleted one everywhere', async () => {
    const answering = recording()
    const other = recording()
    await Effect.runPromise(
      refreshProject(serverWith(mainUri), home, home, projectOf(answering, other), [goneUri]),
    )
    expect(answering.scripts.get(mainUri.toString())).toEqual({ isAssociationDirty: true })
    expect(other.scripts.has(mainUri.toString())).toBe(false)
    expect(answering.deleted).toEqual([goneUri.toString()])
    expect(other.deleted).toEqual([goneUri.toString()])
  })

  it('turns a close into the change it amounts to', () => {
    const folder = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const saved = chapterAt(folder, 'saved.loom', chapter('Saved', 'const saved = 1'))
    const uri = URI.file(saved)
    const gone = URI.file(join(folder, 'corpus', 'gone.loom'))
    const closed = (loom: URI, text: string) =>
      Effect.runSync(
        closeOf(loom, text).pipe(
          Effect.provide(DocumentSource.layer.pipe(Layer.provideMerge(FileSystemSource))),
        ),
      )
    expect(closed(uri, chapter('Saved', 'const saved = 1'))).toEqual([])
    expect(closed(uri, chapter('Saved', 'const saved = 2'))).toEqual([
      { uri: uri.toString(), type: FileChangeType.Changed },
    ])
    expect(closed(gone, chapter('Gone', 'const gone = 1'))).toEqual([
      { uri: gone.toString(), type: FileChangeType.Deleted },
    ])
  })

  it('notes every open loom a configuration change evicts, nested workspaces included', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    configure(join(root, 'nested'), inactive)
    const own = URI.file(join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom'))
    const nested = URI.file(join(root, 'nested', 'corpus', 'note.loom'))
    const outside = URI.file(join(realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-other-'))), 'x.loom'))
    const edited = await Effect.runPromise(Ref.make<ReadonlySet<string>>(new Set()))
    await Effect.runPromise(
      rebuildUnder(serverWith(own, nested, outside), edited, `${root}/`).pipe(
        Effect.provide(
          LoomCompiler.layer.pipe(
            Layer.provide(DocumentSource.layer),
            Layer.provide(LoomDiskCache.layer),
            Layer.provideMerge(FileSystemSource),
            Layer.provide(FileSystemSettings),
            Layer.provide(LoomConfig.layer),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    )
    expect([...(await Effect.runPromise(Ref.get(edited)))].sort()).toEqual(
      [own.fsPath, nested.fsPath].sort(),
    )
  })

  it('holds a request back while a reprojection runs', async () => {
    const answering = recording()
    const { services, projects, reprojecting, edited } = await Effect.runPromise(
      Effect.all({
        services: Cache.make({
          capacity: Infinity,
          lookup: (_: string) => Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>([]),
        }),
        projects: Cache.make({
          capacity: Infinity,
          lookup: (_: string) => Effect.succeed(projectOf(answering, recording())),
        }),
        reprojecting: Semaphore.make(1),
        edited: Ref.make<ReadonlySet<string>>(new Set()),
      }),
    )
    await Effect.runPromise(reprojecting.take(1))
    const answer = Promise.resolve(
      workspaceProject(serverWith(), home, services, projects, reprojecting, edited).getLanguageService(
        mainUri,
      ),
    )
    const waited = await Promise.race([
      answer.then(() => 'answered'),
      new Promise<string>((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve('waiting'), 100)),
    ])
    expect(waited).toBe('waiting')
    await Effect.runPromise(reprojecting.release(1))
    expect<unknown>(await answer).toBe(answering.languageService)
  })

  it('projects a loom the editor has closed from disk, and reads it once', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const text = chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42')
    const closed = URI.file(chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', text))
    const languageService = await projectOver(closedEditor, []).getLanguageService(closed)
    const first = languageService.context.language.scripts.get(closed)
    const again = languageService.context.language.scripts.get(closed)
    expect(first?.snapshot.getText(0, first.snapshot.getLength())).toBe(text)
    expect(first?.generated?.root.snapshot.getText(0, text.length)).toBe(text)
    expect(again?.snapshot).toBe(first?.snapshot)
  })
})

## What the cases draw on

The cases draw on Bun's test runner, and on Node to make the workspaces, start the server, and turn paths into the `file:` addresses the protocol names documents by. Volar's language-server package carries the client half of the protocol too: `createMessageConnection` and the stream reader and writer it speaks through, the `TextEdit` a format answers with, the `CodeAction` a fix arrives as, the `InlayHint` a title beside a number arrives as, the `FoldingRange` a folded box arrives as, the `ApplyWorkspaceEditParams` a request to keep a chapter box in step arrives as, the `CompletionList` a label typed in braces arrives in, and the `FileChangeType` a reported change carries. The language store gives `servicePackage` and `serviceVersion`, which name and version the stand-in. The host runtime gives `runtimeKey`, where the stand-in finds Effect. Effect's `Array` reads the text out of the edits a format returns. The cases that drive the server's parts directly import them from the server module, `projectOver` and `classicTypeScript` among them, and read what `classicTypeScript` loads with Effect's `Result`, and build the caches, the semaphore, and the set those parts take with Effect's `Cache`, `Semaphore`, and `Ref`, run by `Effect`. They take the types of a server, a project, and a service plugin from Volar, and `URI` names their looms. To run `rebuildUnder` they assemble the compiler the way the server does, from `LoomCompiler`, `DocumentSource`, `WorkspaceSettings`, and `LoomConfig`, with Effect's `Layer`. To run `closeOf` they add the `FileSystemSource` layer.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import {
  type ApplyWorkspaceEditParams,
  type CodeAction,
  type CompletionList,
  createMessageConnection,
  type Diagnostic,
  FileChangeType,
  type FoldingRange,
  type InlayHint,
  StreamMessageReader,
  StreamMessageWriter,
  type TextEdit,
} from '@volar/language-server/node'
import { Array, Cache, Effect, Layer, Ref, Result, Semaphore } from 'effect'
import { LoomConfig } from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import { DocumentSource, LoomCompiler } from '../src/LoomCompiler'
import { FileSystemSettings } from '../src/WorkspaceSettings'
import { LoomDiskCache } from '../src/LoomDiskCache'
import { FileSystemSource } from '../src/WorkspaceSource'
import type { LanguageServer, LanguageServerProject, LanguageServicePlugin } from '@volar/language-server/node'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import {
  classicTypeScript,
  closeOf,
  projectOver,
  rebuildUnder,
  refreshProject,
  workspaceProject,
} from '../src/LoomServer'
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process'
import { mkdirSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, realpathSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'
import { servicePackage, serviceVersion } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LoomStore'
import { runtimeKey } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/Runtime'

